## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design in-place sorting algorithms, understand stability, and reason about trade-offs between time and memory in constrained environments.

## Key Concepts

- In-place merging
- Stability
- Time/space complexity
- Rotation-based merging

## Code Example

```javascript
function inPlaceMerge(arr, left, mid, right) {
  let i = left, j = mid + 1;
  while (i <= mid && j <= right) {
    if (arr[i] <= arr[j]) { i++; continue; }
    const tmp = arr[j];
    for (let k = j; k > i; k--) arr[k] = arr[k - 1];
    arr[i] = tmp;
    i++; mid++; j++;
  }
}

function inPlaceMergeSort(arr, left = 0, right = arr.length - 1) {
  if (left >= right) return;
  const mid = Math.floor((left + right) / 2);
  inPlaceMergeSort(arr, left, mid);
  inPlaceMergeSort(arr, mid + 1, right);
  inPlaceMerge(arr, left, mid, right);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you optimize rotations to guarantee O(n log n) time in all cases?
- How does memory locality affect performance compared with standard mergesort?

## Why This Is Asked
Beginner DP concepts with a new angle: counting unordered combinations (coin change ways) and contrasting memoization with tabulation.

## Key Concepts
- Dynamic programming (top-down memoization)
- Tabulation (bottom-up DP)
- Unordered combinations vs permutations
- Space optimization (1D DP)

## Code Example
```javascript
function countWaysTopDown(coins, target) {
  const n = coins.length;
  const memo = new Map();
  function dfs(i, rem) {
    if (rem === 0) return 1;
    if (rem < 0 || i === n) return 0;
    const key = i + '|' + rem;
    if (memo.has(key)) return memo.get(key);
    const res = dfs(i + 1, rem) + dfs(i, rem - coins[i]);
    memo.set(key, res);
    return res;
  }
  return dfs(0, target);
}

function countWaysBottomUp(coins, target) {
  const dp = new Array(target + 1).fill(0);
  dp[0] = 1;
  for (const c of coins) {
    for (let r = c; r <= target; r++) {
      dp[r] += dp[r - c];
    }
  }
  return dp[target];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you count permutations (order matters) instead of combinations?
- How would you handle very large targets or mod results to avoid overflow in languages with fixed integer sizes?

## Why This Is Asked

Duplicates are common in real data and inputs are often skewed. A 3-way partition quicksort directly handles the middle bucket of equal keys, avoiding repeated swaps of equal elements and preventing degeneracy.

## Key Concepts

- 3-way partitioning (Dutch national flag style) for handling duplicates
- Pivot selection strategies (median-of-three to reduce worst-case risk)
- Introsort/hybrid switching to guarantee worst-case bounds
- In-place sorting with O(log n) recursion depth

## Code Example

```javascript
function threeWayQuicksort(a, lo = 0, hi = a.length - 1) {
  while (lo < hi) {
    let lt = lo, i = lo + 1, gt = hi;
    const pivot = a[lo];
    while (i <= gt) {
      if (a[i] < pivot) { [a[lt], a[i]] = [a[i], a[lt]]; lt++; i++; }
      else if (a[i] > pivot) { [a[i], a[gt]] = [a[gt], a[i]]; gt--; }
      else { i++; }
    }
    threeWayQuicksort(a, lo, lt - 1);
    lo = gt; // tail recursion on the right side
  }
  return a;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you modify the pivot strategy to further reduce worst-case risk?
- How would you adapt this to a non-recursive implementation to save stack depth?
- Compare in-place 3-way quicksort with a stable sort like mergesort in terms of cache efficiency and memory usage.

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes understanding of introsort-style hybrids, balancing average performance with worst-case guarantees in production environments. It tests pivot strategies, partitioning, depth tracking, and in-place constraints.

## Key Concepts

- Introsort: hybrid of quicksort and heapsort with depth-based switch
- Median-of-three pivot: reduces chance of worst-case partitions
- 3-way partitioning: handles duplicates efficiently
- Insertion sort for tiny subarrays: amortized gains

## Code Example

```javascript
function introsort(arr) {
  const maxDepth = Math.floor(Math.log2(arr.length)) * 2;
  function insertionSort(a, lo, hi) {
    for (let i = lo + 1; i < hi; i++) {
      let key = a[i], j = i - 1;
      while (j >= lo && a[j] > key) { a[j + 1] = a[j]; j--; }
      a[j + 1] = key;
    }
  }
  function heapify(a, count, i, offset) {
    // placeholder for in-place heapify within [offset, offset+count)
  }
  function partition(a, lo, hi) {
    // median-of-three and 3-way partitioning placeholder
    return Math.floor((lo + hi) / 2);
  }
  function sort(a, lo, hi, depth) {
    if (hi - lo <= 16) { insertionSort(a, lo, hi); return; }
    if (depth > maxDepth) { /* heapsort(a, lo, hi); */ return; }
    const pivot = partition(a, lo, hi);
    // recursively sort partitions
    sort(a, lo, pivot, depth + 1);
    sort(a, pivot, hi, depth + 1);
  }
  sort(arr, 0, arr.length, 0);
}
```

